{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1779720883,"nonce":"HMX94EcC9cqg7qSAYcSkF56UiDo=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABDlFXJA","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/88#issuecomment-4535179044"},"message":"## Data-derived gaps from the §2 opportunistic analysis\n\nThe post-mission §2 characterization ([PR #172](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/pull/172), pooling 7 deployments) pinned what logged survey data allows but left gaps that controlled #88 runs are the right tool for. Concrete targets:\n\n1. **Intermediate PWM→speed curve (1550–1750)** — opportunistic data lacks heading diversity at fixed sub-cruise throttle, so the circle fit only resolves cruise (~1.52 m/s @ 1750–1850) and full (~1.9 m/s @ 1950–2000). **Steady holds at 1600/1650/1700/1750** would fill the curve.\n2. **Clean high-throttle tail** — full-throttle fits varied (1.33–1.94 m/s) from heading-arc/fit-quality, not boat speed. **Deliberate reciprocal legs** (opposing headings, same throttle, back-to-back) give a clean max STW + a direct current vector.\n3. **Straight-reverse speed** — the boat never holds a straight reverse heading in survey data; peak ~1.4 m/s seen only briefly. Need **sustained straight full-reverse holds**.\n4. **Active crash-stop braking distance** — coast-down (passive) is ~10–15 m from cruise; the fast-stop number requires a **forward-cruise → full-reverse** maneuver, which is the real braking authority and is uncharacterized.\n\nReciprocal-pair legs also double as the gold-standard current cross-check (the §2 circle-fit currents validated against NOAA ACT0731 but direction was approximate at the pier).\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null}]}